     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Digoxin may also be used for rate control in AF with HFrEF or HFpEF."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "HF dose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " 0.125–0.25 mg PO daily (full effect in about 1 week); a loading dose is rarely needed in HF; with abnormal renal function 0.125 mg Mon/Wed/Fri."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Interactions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " digoxin is a P-gp substrate, so P-gp inhibitors/inducers change its levels; amiodarone raises digoxin levels: halve the digoxin dose (or stop it) and watch for toxicity; check a level before starting an interacting drug."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Serum level:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " <0.5 ng/mL loses efficacy and >2 ng/mL adds toxicity without benefit; draw just before the next dose or ≥6 h after the last dose, and don't change the dose on one isolated level."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Toxicity:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " nausea, vomiting, visual changes and arrhythmias; bidirectional VT, nonparoxysmal junctional tachycardia in AF and hyperkalemia are serious signs; hypokalemia or hypomagnesemia allows toxicity at lower levels. Stop digoxin, monitor, keep K 4.0–5.5; Digoxin Immune Fab for life-threatening arrhythmias or hyperkalemia; atropine for bradycardia/heart block; not removed by dialysis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Counsel:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " report nausea, vomiting, persistent diarrhea, confusion, weakness or visual changes (blurred or green-yellow vision, halos), which can mean the dose is too high."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Verapamil can raise digoxin levels 50–75% in the first week; monitor digoxin levels when starting, adjusting or stopping diltiazem."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
